  "resultsInfoTitle": "About these scores",
  "resultsInfoBody": "Each bar is the most recent result for that test, shown as a level of concern from 0% to 100%. Higher always means more worth looking at, whichever test it came from.\n\nThat needs saying because the tests do not all count the same way. On the tapping or tremor tests a high raw score means more sign of tremor; on the memory and reasoning batteries a high raw score means you did well. Both are converted here, so a low bar is always the calmer one.\n\nThe three bands — reassuring, borderline and notable — are provisional cut-offs, not clinical thresholds. A single notable result on one day means far less than the same result appearing again and again across weeks.\n\nTap a test to see how it has changed over time.",

  "overallTrend": "Overall trend",
  "trendInfoTitle": "About the trend",
  "trendInfoBody": "One point per day on which you took at least one test, averaged across every test you took that day.\n\nUse it to see direction rather than detail: a line drifting upwards over several weeks is worth mentioning to your doctor, while a single spike usually says more about a tired hand, a rushed attempt or a noisy room than about your health.\n\nDays with no tests are simply missing from the line, so a stretch of activity followed by a gap will look like one long step. Taking tests at roughly the same time of day makes the comparison fairer.",
